armacraft perf patch 0003 -- UNAPPLIED. Protocol feature `delta` (guest side) + PROTOCOL.md.

Why: every `state` re-sends every wall at 30 Hz. In measured AI rounds only ~8% of walls change per
frame (head walls + new holes), and cycles are ~20% of the bytes.

What it does (opt-in, v1 stays the default):
- guest hello advertises "features":["delta"]; a host that sends {"type":"hello","features":["delta"]}
  gets `delta` lines instead of `state`: all cycles, only walls that are new/changed since what *this
  host* was last sent, and `removed` ids. key:true on opt-in and at every round start. A later host
  hello replaces the feature set ("features":[] switches back to `state`).
- diffing is against the per-host "last sent" map (exact REAL compare, no formatting for unchanged
  walls), so a delta skipped for a slow host (backlog) is folded into the next one.
- a head wall has no net id yet (id 0 in `state`, one per alive cycle); deltas carry it as -cycle.
- the frame (cycles + visible walls) is built once per emission and shared by all hosts; `state`
  output is produced by the same formatting code as before (byte layout unchanged), and is now only
  built when some host actually takes it.
Hosts that never send a hello, and the v1 host in host/, see no difference.

Checked: built (docker build of guest/ + this patch, tag armacraft-guest:perf-delta, g++ -std=c++11,
no warnings in gBridge.cpp); `tools/bench_guest.py deltacheck` (one v1 and one delta connection to
the same guest, frames matched by `t`): folded deltas == v1 state walls on 3567/3567 frames incl. a
round change (8 AIs) with this exact patch, and on 2698 (32 AIs) + 4444 (8 AIs) frames with the
previous revision (same diff logic, hello could not switch back); deltas were 24-26% of the v1 bytes. Interleaved CPU A/B on one 32-AI round (~270 walls, 4 windows each): snapshot
cost 0.90 ms (v1 state) vs 0.36 ms (delta) of guest CPU per snapshot.
Base: guest/src/gBridge.cpp as of 2026-10-02 00:39 BST, PROTOCOL.md as of 2026-10-01 22:50. The tree
that was built and measured differed from this base only in one log string ("bridge: listening on").
